Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may only effect a Company Adverse Recommendation Change if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company\u2019s outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, that the failure to make such a change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of its intention to consider an Acquisition Proposal that it deems to be a Superior Proposal, and (iii) the Company, after taking into account the legal, reputational, and financial aspects of the Acquisition Proposal, determines that such proposal remains superior. The Company must also consider any potential adverse effects on the Company\u2019s stakeholders and its long-term strategic goals, in addition to the financial terms, before finalizing its decision.\nAnswer:
